%U A402101 3,3,3,2,3,3,2,2,3,2,2,2,2,2,2,1,2,2,1,1,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,0,1,1,0,0,1
%N A402101 Number of ways to earn a score of 1.5*n on the AMC 10 or AMC 12, distinct up to the number of questions answered correctly, left blank, or answered incorrectly. See comments.
%C A402101 The AMC 10 and AMC 12 are competition mathematics tests administered annually to high school students in the United States. Both are 75-minute, 25 multiple choice question exams with five choices per question. Since 2007, the scoring system has been as follows: 6 points for every question answered correctly, 1.5 points for every question left blank, and 0 points for every question answered incorrectly.
%C A402101 It follows that a(n) is the number of triples (x,y,z) of nonnegative integers satisfying x + y + z = 25 and 4*x + y = n.
%C A402101 Since the score earned must be a multiple of 1.5 between 0 and 150, this sequence only lists a(n) for 0 <= n <= 100. For any other value of n, a(n) is zero. The only unattainable scores between 0 and 150 that are multiples of 1.5 are 142.5, 147, and 148.5 (n = 95, 98, 99).
%C A402101 See A402102 for a different approach to counting ways of earning a certain score.
%H A402101 Aidan Markey, <a href="/A402101/a402101.pdf">Derivation of formula</a>.
%H A402101 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Mathematics_Competitions">American Mathematics Competitions</a>.
%F A402101 a(n) = floor(n/4) - max(8, floor((n+1)/3)) + 9.
%F A402101 If n <= 25, a(n) = floor(n/4) + 1.
%F A402101 If n > 25, a(n) = floor(n/4) - floor((n+1)/3) + 9.
%e A402101 For n = 60 (a score of 90), there are four valid combinations (#correct, #blank, #incorrect): (12,12,1), (13,8,4), (14,4,7), (15,0,10).
%t A402101 A402101[n_] := Quotient[n, 4] + If[n <= 25, 1, 9 - Quotient[n + 1, 3]];
%t A402101 Array[A402101, 101, 0] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 23 2026 *)
%Y A402101 Cf. A402102.
%K A402101 nonn,easy,fini,full,new
%O A402101 0,5
